# Code owners. With the repository ruleset asking for code-owner review, a pull request that touches one
# of these paths needs its owner's approval before it can merge. GitHub does not let an author approve their
# own pull request, so the owner merges their own through the ruleset's bypass list.
#
# The owner owns everything: the paid evaluation workflows build and run the whole app and its tests from
# `main` with provider keys, so any merged change can reach them. The lines after it are the places where a
# change runs with a secret, ships to users or decides what CI trusts, kept so they stay visible and so the
# `.github/` entry that Tools/check_workflow_security.py requires has a reason to exist.

